{:edb/db-before-t 1921 :edb/db-after-t 1922 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535234 50 #inst "2026-10-13T07:19:24.704Z" 13194139535234 17592186045417 true] [17592186054774 1038 #edb/ref 17592186484207 13194139535234 17592186045417 true] [17592186054774 1038 #edb/ref 17592186484257 13194139535234 17592186045417 true] [17592186054775 1038 #edb/ref 17592186484273 13194139535234 17592186045417 true] [17592186061266 1078 #edb/ref 17592186061267 13194139535234 17592186045417 true] [17592186064297 1038 #edb/ref 17592186484259 13194139535234 17592186045417 true] [17592186064314 1038 #edb/ref 17592186484225 13194139535234 17592186045417 true] [17592186064314 1038 #edb/ref 17592186484262 13194139535234 17592186045417 true] [17592186064315 1038 #edb/ref 17592186484253 13194139535234 17592186045417 true] [17592186064506 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535234 17592186045417 true] [17592186064506 1071 "Let $X_{1}, X_{2}, …, X_{25}$ be a random sample of size $n = 25$ from a population with probability density function\n$f(x) = {\\frac{3}{8}x^{2},\\begin{vmatrix}0 \\le x \\le 2, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$.\nGiven that the population variance $σ^{2} = 0.15$, calculate $P(X^(―) > 1.6)$.\n\n*Hint: Assume that the sample size is sufficiently large for the central limit theorem to be applied. A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $z$ | $0.07$ | $0.08$ | $0.09$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $1.1$ | $0.8790$ | $0.8810$ | $0.8830$ |\n| $1.2$ | $0.8980$ | $0.8997$ | $0.9015$ |\n| $1.3$ | $0.9147$ | $0.9162$ | $0.9177$ |\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139535234 17592186045417 false] [17592186064506 1071 "Let ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{25}$ be a random sample of size $n=25$ from a population with probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{8}{x}^{2}, & 0\\le x\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=0.15,$ calculate $P(\\overset{―}{X}>1.6).$\n\n*Hint: Assume that the sample size is sufficiently large for the central limit theorem to be applied. A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ | $\\,0.08\\,$ | $\\,0.09\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,1.1\\,$ | $\\,0.8790\\,$ | $\\,0.8810\\,$ | $\\,0.8830\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.2\\,$ | $\\,0.8980\\,$ | $\\,0.8997\\,$ | $\\,0.9015\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.3\\,$ | $\\,0.9147\\,$ | $\\,0.9162\\,$ | $\\,0.9177\\,$ |" 13194139535234 17592186045417 true] [17592186064506 1074 "Suppose that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population standard deviation $\\sigma .$ The central limit theorem (or CLT) states that for sufficiently large $n,$ the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ can be approximated as \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case:\n\n- the sample size is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =25,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\text{E}[X] \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }x\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}x\\cdot \\frac{3}{8}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{3}{8}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{3{x}^{4}}{32}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =1.5,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- the population variance is ${\\sigma }^{2}=0.15.$\n\nTherefore, by the CLT, we have that the distribution of the sample mean can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N\\,(1.5,\\frac{0.15}{25}) \\\\  & ∼N(1.5,0.006).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(\\overset{―}{X}>1.6).$ So, we convert $\\overset{―}{X}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{―}{X}>1.6) & =P(Z>\\frac{1.6-1.5}{\\sqrt{0.006}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z>1.29)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the complement of the normal distribution, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z>1.29) & =1-P(Z<1.29) \\\\  & =1-Φ(1.29).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(1.29)=0.9015.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{―}{X}>1.6) & =1-Φ(1.29) \\\\  & =1-0.9015 \\\\  & =0.0985.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535234 17592186045417 true] [17592186066498 1075 #edb/ref 17592186066499 13194139535234 17592186045417 false] [17592186066498 1075 #edb/ref 17592186066500 13194139535234 17592186045417 false] [17592186066498 1075 #edb/ref 17592186066501 13194139535234 17592186045417 false] [17592186066498 1075 #edb/ref 17592186066502 13194139535234 17592186045417 false] [17592186066498 1075 #edb/ref 17592186066503 13194139535234 17592186045417 false] [17592186066498 1075 #edb/ref 17592186483599 13194139535234 17592186045417 true] [17592186066498 1075 #edb/ref 17592186483653 13194139535234 17592186045417 true] [17592186066498 1075 #edb/ref 17592186483722 13194139535234 17592186045417 true] [17592186066498 1075 #edb/ref 17592186483874 13194139535234 17592186045417 true] [17592186066498 1075 #edb/ref 17592186483970 13194139535234 17592186045417 true] [17592186066498 1078 #edb/ref 17592186483970 13194139535234 17592186045417 true] [17592186067732 1075 #edb/ref 17592186067735 13194139535234 17592186045417 false] [17592186067732 1075 #edb/ref 17592186067736 13194139535234 17592186045417 false] [17592186067732 1075 #edb/ref 17592186067737 13194139535234 17592186045417 false] [17592186067732 1075 #edb/ref 17592186483915 13194139535234 17592186045417 true] [17592186067732 1075 #edb/ref 17592186483948 13194139535234 17592186045417 true] [17592186067732 1075 #edb/ref 17592186484084 13194139535234 17592186045417 true] [17592186067732 1078 #edb/ref 17592186483915 13194139535234 17592186045417 true] [17592186067924 1075 #edb/ref 17592186067925 13194139535234 17592186045417 false] [17592186067924 1075 #edb/ref 17592186067926 13194139535234 17592186045417 false] [17592186067924 1075 #edb/ref 17592186067927 13194139535234 17592186045417 false] [17592186067924 1075 #edb/ref 17592186067928 13194139535234 17592186045417 false] [17592186067924 1075 #edb/ref 17592186483658 13194139535234 17592186045417 true] [17592186067924 1075 #edb/ref 17592186483969 13194139535234 17592186045417 true] [17592186067924 1075 #edb/ref 17592186484012 13194139535234 17592186045417 true] [17592186067924 1075 #edb/ref 17592186484081 13194139535234 17592186045417 true] [17592186067924 1078 #edb/ref 17592186483658 13194139535234 17592186045417 true] [17592186067990 1078 #edb/ref 17592186067993 13194139535234 17592186045417 true] [17592186068437 1074 "In general, the fractional exponent $x^{1/n}$ can also be written as the $n$th root $\\sqrt[n]{x}$. In this particular case, $n=3$, $x=2a$, and we have\n\n$$\n(2a)^{1/3} = \\sqrt[3]{2a}\n$$" 13194139535234 17592186045417 false] [17592186068437 1074 "In general, the fractional exponent $x^{1/n}$ can also be written as the $n$th root $\\sqrt[n]{x}.$ In this particular case, $n=3,$ $x=2a,$ and we have\n\n$$\n(2a)^{1/3} = \\sqrt[3]{2a}\n$$" 13194139535234 17592186045417 true] [17592186068438 1074 "In general, the $n$th root $\\sqrt[n]{x}$ can also be written using a fractional exponent as $x^{1/n}$. In this particular case, $n=4$, $x=2y$, and we have\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[4]{2y} = (2y)^{1/4}\n$$" 13194139535234 17592186045417 false] [17592186068438 1074 "In general, the $n$th root $\\sqrt[n]{x}$ can also be written using a fractional exponent as $x^{1/n}.$ In this particular case, $n=4,$ $x=2y,$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[4]{2y} = (2y)^{1/4}\n$$" 13194139535234 17592186045417 true] [17592186068440 1074 "Remember that, in general, a negative exponent can be converted to a positive exponent if we take the reciprocal. Here, we have\n\n$$\nx^{-1/2} = \\dfrac{1}{x^{1/2}}\n$$\n\nNow, we can proceed as usual to convert the fractional exponent to a radical\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{x^{1/2}} = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nx^{-1/2} = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\n$$" 13194139535234 17592186045417 false] [17592186068440 1074 "Remember that, in general, a negative exponent can be converted to a positive exponent if we take the reciprocal. Here, we have\n\n$$\nx^{-1/2} = \\dfrac{1}{x^{1/2}}.\n$$\n\nNow, we can proceed as usual to convert the fractional exponent to a radical\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{x^{1/2}} = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nx^{-1/2} =  \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}.\n$$" 13194139535234 17592186045417 true] [17592186068441 1071 "Express $\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt[3\\phantom{\\mid}]{2y}}$ using power notation." 13194139535234 17592186045417 false] [17592186068441 1071 "Express $\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt[3\\phantom{|}]{2y}}$ using power notation." 13194139535234 17592186045417 true] [17592186068441 1074 "First, we convert the radical to a fractional exponent:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt[3\\phantom{\\mid}]{2y}} = \\dfrac{1}{(2y)^{1/3}}\n$$\n\nThen, we take the reciprocal and convert the exponent to a negative exponent:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{(2y)^{1/3}} = (2y)^{-1/3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt[3\\phantom{\\mid}]{2y}} = (2y)^{-1/3}\n$$" 13194139535234 17592186045417 false] [17592186068441 1074 "First, we convert the radical to a fractional exponent:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt[3\\phantom{|}]{2y}} = \\dfrac{1}{(2y)^{1/3}}\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, we take the reciprocal and convert the exponent to a negative exponent:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{(2y)^{1/3}} = (2y)^{-1/3}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt[3\\phantom{|}]{2y}} = (2y)^{-1/3}\n$$" 13194139535234 17592186045417 true] [17592186068464 1071 "Factor $9x^2+30x+25$." 13194139535234 17592186045417 false] [17592186068464 1071 "Factor $9x^2+30x+25.$" 13194139535234 17592186045417 true] [17592186068464 1074 "The \"square of a sum\" formula states that\n\n$$\na^2 + {\\color{red}{2}}a{\\color{blue}{b}} + {\\color{blue}b}^2 =  (a+{\\color{blue}{b}})^2 .\n$$\n\nWe will use this formula to factor the given trinomial. Our trinomial is\n\n$$\n9x^2+30x+25.\n$$\n\nFirst, we write both the quadratic term and the constant term as perfect squares:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3x)^{2}+30x+(5)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we factor a $2$ out of the linear term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3x)^{2}+2(15x)+(5)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we write the middle parentheses $(15x)$ as a product of $3x$ and ${\\color{blue}5}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3x)^{2}+2(3x\\cdot 5)+(5)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we factor the trinomial using the square of sum formula with $a=3x$ and ${\\color{blue}{b}} = {\\color{blue}{5}}.$ This gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3x+5)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535234 17592186045417 true] [17592186068464 1074 "The \"square of a sum\" formula states that\n\n$$\na^2 + {\\color{red}{2}}a{\\color{blue}{b}} + {\\color{blue}b}^2 = (a+{\\color{blue}{b}})^2\n$$\n\nWe will use this formula to factor the given trinomial. Our trinomial is\n\n$$\n9x^2+30x+25\n$$\n\nFirst, we write both the quadratic term and the constant term as perfect squares:\n\n$$\n(3x)^{2} + 30x + (5)^{2}\n$$\n\nNext, we factor a $2$ out of the linear term:\n$(3x)^{2} + 2(15x) + (5)^{2}$\n\nThen, we write the middle parentheses $(15x)$ as a product of $3x$ and ${\\color{blue}5}$:\n$(3x)^{2} + 2(3x \\cdot 5) + (5)^{2}$\n\nFinally, we factor the trinomial using the square of sum formula with $a=3x$ and\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{b}} = {\\color{blue}{5}}\n$$\n\nThis gives\n$(3x + 5)^{2}$" 13194139535234 17592186045417 false] [17592186068465 1071 "Factor the expression $7a^2+28a+28$." 13194139535234 17592186045417 false] [17592186068465 1071 "Factor the expression $7a^2+28a+28.$" 13194139535234 17592186045417 true] [17592186068465 1074 "Before we start our normal process of factoring, notice that all of the terms have a common factor of $7$. Therefore, we can simplify the expression by factoring out $7$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n7a^{2} + 28a + 28 &=  \\\\\n&= 7(a^{2} + 4a + 4)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's factor the expression inside parentheses using the square of sum formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na^{2} + 4a + 4 &=  \\\\\n&= a^{2} + 4a + 2^{2} \\\\\n&= a^{2} + 2 \\cdot 2a + 2^{2} \\\\\n&= (a + 2)^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our original expression factors as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n7(a^{2} + 4a + 4) &=  \\\\\n&= 7(a + 2)^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535234 17592186045417 false] [17592186068465 1074 "Before we start our normal process of factoring, notice that all of the terms have a common factor of $7.$ Therefore, we can simplify the expression by factoring out $7,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7{a}^{2}+28a+28 & = \\\\ 7({a}^{2}+4a+4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's factor the expression inside parentheses using the square of sum formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}^{2}+4a+4 & = \\\\ {a}^{2}+4a+{2}^{2} \\\\ {a}^{2}+2\\cdot 2a+{2}^{2} \\\\ (a+2)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our original expression factors as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7({a}^{2}+4a+4) & = \\\\ 7(a+2)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535234 17592186045417 true] [17592186068466 1071 "Factor ![](images/a0/a026f57159735d92a77f9fe46f17720e60e27081143e9fa0e128a69ca1e56687.svg)" 13194139535234 17592186045417 true] [17592186068466 1071 "Factor $-16y^2 + 4y - \\dfrac{1}{4}$." 13194139535234 17592186045417 false] [17592186068466 1074 "Notice that the leading coefficient is negative. To reduce the expression to a more familiar form, we can factor out ![](images/66/66ef4b5796888b4025ee2d8b3d516ec80741b341db9389696b6e77a4af5208d8.svg) as follows:\n\n![](images/19/1950433407a5924a7809709ff5239c25e9132f229b4aa157d0af383d1f08b30c.svg)\n\nNow, we factor the quadratic expression inside the parentheses using the square of a difference formula:\n\n![](images/ba/baa2bc75c152c9a312cd1c09da0ac322782577892fc11679d8b5277a38ab627f.svg)\n\nTherefore, our original expression factors as follows:\n\n![](images/a0/a0fc84a06635785af990620d5d671329588a8018c0e0cdbc6beb130a23596070.svg)" 13194139535234 17592186045417 true] [17592186068466 1074 "Notice that the leading coefficient is negative. To reduce the expression to a more familiar form, we can factor out $-1$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-16y^{2} + 4y - \\frac{1}{4} &=  \\\\\n&= - (16y^{2} - 4y + \\frac{1}{4})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the quadratic expression inside the parentheses using the sum of difference formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n16y^{2} - 4y + \\frac{1}{4} &=  \\\\\n(4y)^{2} - 4y + (\\frac{1}{2})^{2} &=  \\\\\n(4y)^{2} - 2 \\cdot (2y) + (\\frac{1}{2})^{2} &=  \\\\\n(4y)^{2} - 2 \\cdot (4y \\cdot \\frac{1}{2}) + (\\frac{1}{2})^{2} &=  \\\\\n&= (4y - \\frac{1}{2})^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our original expression factors as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n- (16y^{2} - 4y + \\frac{1}{4}) &=  \\\\\n&= - (4y - \\frac{1}{2})^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535234 17592186045417 false] [17592186068467 1071 "Find $\\sqrt[3]{-8x^6}$." 13194139535234 17592186045417 false] [17592186068467 1071 "Find $\\sqrt[3]{-8x^6}.$" 13194139535234 17592186045417 true] [17592186068467 1074 "We write the term under the radical as a perfect cube, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt[3]{-8x^{6}} &=  \\\\\n\\sqrt[3]{(-2x^{2}) \\cdot (-2x^{2}) \\cdot (-2x^{2})} &=  \\\\\n\\sqrt[3]{(-2x^{2})^{3}} &=  \\\\\n&= - 2x^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535234 17592186045417 false] [17592186068467 1074 "We write the term under the radical as a perfect cube, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{-8{x}^{6}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{(-2{x}^{2})\\cdot (-2{x}^{2})\\cdot (-2{x}^{2})} & = \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{(-2{x}^{2})^{3}} & = \\\\ -2{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535234 17592186045417 true] [17592186068468 1071 "Simplify $\\sqrt[3]{\\dfrac{a^3}{(b-1)^3}}$." 13194139535234 17592186045417 false] [17592186068468 1071 "Simplify $\\sqrt[3]{\\dfrac{a^3}{(b-1)^3}}.$" 13194139535234 17592186045417 true] [17592186068468 1074 "We write the term under the radical as a perfect cube, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt[3]{(a^{3})/((b - 1)^{3})} &=  \\\\\n\\sqrt[3]{(a)/((b - 1)) \\cdot (a)/((b - 1)) \\cdot (a)/((b - 1))} &=  \\\\\n\\sqrt[3]{(\\frac{a}{b - 1})^{3}} &=  \\\\\n&= \\frac{a}{b - 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535234 17592186045417 false] [17592186068468 1074 "We write the term under the radical as a perfect cube, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{{a}^{3}}{(b-1)^{3}}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{a}{(b-1)}\\cdot \\frac{a}{(b-1)}\\cdot \\frac{a}{(b-1)}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{{(\\frac{a}{b-1})}^{3}} & = \\\\ \\frac{a}{b-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535234 17592186045417 true] [17592186068520 1071 "Factor $9y^2-12y+4$." 13194139535234 17592186045417 false] [17592186068520 1071 "Factor $9y^2-12y+4.$" 13194139535234 17592186045417 true] [17592186068520 1074 "The \"square of a difference\" formula states that\n\n$$\na^2 - {\\color{red}{2}}a{\\color{blue}{b}} + {\\color{blue}b}^2 =  (a-{\\color{blue}{b}})^2 .\n$$\n\nWe will use this formula to factor the given trinomial. Our trinomial is\n\n$$\n9y^2-12y+4.\n$$\n\nFirst, we write both the quadratic term and the constant term as perfect squares:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3y)^{2}-12y+(2)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we factor a $2$ out of the linear term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3y)^{2}-2(6y)+(2)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we write the middle parentheses $(6y)$ as a product of $3y$ and ${\\color{blue}2}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3y)^{2}-2(3y\\cdot 2)+(2)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we factor the trinomial using the square of difference formula with $a=3y$ and ${\\color{blue}{b}} = {\\color{blue}{2}}.$ This gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3y-2)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535234 17592186045417 true] [17592186068520 1074 "The \"square of a difference\" formula states that\n\n$$\na^2 - {\\color{red}{2}}a{\\color{blue}{b}} + {\\color{blue}b}^2 = (a-{\\color{blue}{b}})^2\n$$\n\nWe will use this formula to factor the given trinomial. Our trinomial is\n\n$$\n9y^2-12y+4\n$$\n\nFirst, we write both the quadratic term and the constant term as perfect squares:\n$(3y)^{2} - 12y + (2)^{2}$\n\nNext, we factor a $2$ out of the linear term:\n$(3y)^{2} - 2(6y) + (2)^{2}$\n\nThen, we write the middle parentheses $(6y)$ as a product of $3y$ and ${\\color{blue}2}$:\n$(3y)^{2} - 2(3y \\cdot 2) + (2)^{2}$\n\nFinally, we factor the trinomial using the square of difference formula with $a=3y$ and\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{b}} = {\\color{blue}{2}}\n$$\n\nThis gives\n$(3y - 2)^{2}$" 13194139535234 17592186045417 false] [17592186068624 1071 "Which of the following values of $x$ satisfy the inequality $2x < 10\\,$?\n\n1. $x=0$\n2. $x=2$\n3. $x=5$" 13194139535234 17592186045417 false] [17592186068624 1071 "Which of the following values of $x$ satisfy the inequality $2x < 10\\,?$\n\nI. $x=0$\n\nII. $x=2$\n\nIII. $x=5$" 13194139535234 17592186045417 true] [17592186068624 1074 "We substitute each value into the given inequality and check to see if the resulting statement is true or false.\n\n- We substitute the value ${\\color{blue}x}={\\color{blue}0}$ into the given inequality. We get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & <10 \\\\ 2\\cdot x & <10 \\\\ 2\\cdot 0 & <10 \\\\ 0 & <10\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, substituting $x=0$ into the inequality leads to a **true** statement. \n\nTherefore, $x=0$ **satisfies** this inequality.\n\n- We substitute the value ${\\color{blue}x}={\\color{blue}2}$ into the given inequality. We get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & <10 \\\\ 2\\cdot x & <10 \\\\ 2\\cdot 2 & <10 \\\\ 4 & <10\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, substituting $x=2$ into the inequality leads to a **true** statement. \n\nTherefore, $x=2$ **satisfies** this inequality.\n\n- We substitute the value ${\\color{blue}x}={\\color{blue}5}$ into the given inequality. We get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & <10 \\\\ 2\\cdot x & <10 \\\\ 2\\