25$." 13194139535234 17592186045417 false] [17592186069220 1071 "Factor $25{y}^{2}+50y+25.$" 13194139535234 17592186045417 true] [17592186069220 1074 "Notice that all of the terms have a common factor of $25.$ Therefore, we can simplify the expression by factoring out $25,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}25{y}^{2}+50y+25 & = \\\\ 25({y}^{2}+2y+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's factor the expression inside parentheses using the square of a sum formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}+2y+1 & = \\\\ {y}^{2}+2y+{1}^{2} & = \\\\ {y}^{2}+2\\cdot y+{1}^{2} & = \\\\ (y+1)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our original expression factors as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}25({y}^{2}+2y+1) & = \\\\ 25(y+1)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535234 17592186045417 true] [17592186069427 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535234 17592186045417 true] [17592186069427 1071 "Simplify $\\sqrt[3]{(2x - 1)^{3}}$." 13194139535234 17592186045417 false] [17592186069427 1071 "Simplify $\\sqrt[3]{(2x-1)^{3}}.$" 13194139535234 17592186045417 true] [17592186069427 1074 "The expression underneath the radical is already a perfect cube. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{(2x-1)^{3}} & =2x-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535234 17592186045417 true] [17592186069459 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535234 17592186045417 true] [17592186069459 1071 "$(5k)^{-1/3}=$" 13194139535234 17592186045417 true] [17592186069459 1071 "What is $(5k)^{-1/3}$?" 13194139535234 17592186045417 false] [17592186069459 1074 "Remember that in general, a negative exponent can be converted to a positive exponent if we take the reciprocal. Here, we have:\n\n$$\n(5k)^{-1/3}=\\frac{1}{(5k)^{1/3}}\n$$\n\nNow, we can proceed as usual to convert the fractional exponent to a radical:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{(5k)^{1/3}}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{5k}}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n$$\n(5k)^{-1/3}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{5k}}\n$$" 13194139535234 17592186045417 true] [17592186069470 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535234 17592186045417 true] [17592186069470 1071 "Which of the following values of $a$ satisfy the inequality $-3 \\le 2a + 1 < 8$?\n\n1. $a =-2$\n2. $a = 2$\n3. $a = 5$" 13194139535234 17592186045417 false] [17592186069470 1071 "Which of the following values of $a$ satisfy the inequality $-3\\le 2a+1<8\\,?$\n\nI. $a=-2$\n\nII. $a=2$\n\nIII. $a=5$" 13194139535234 17592186045417 true] [17592186069470 1074 "We substitute each value into the given inequality and check to see if the resulting statement is true or false.\n\n- We substitute the value $a=-2$ into the given inequality. We get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3\\le 2a+1 & <8 \\\\ -3\\le 2\\cdot (-2)+1 & <8 \\\\ -3\\le -3 & <8\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, substituting $a=-2$ into the inequality leads to a **true** statement. \n\nTherefore, $a=-2$ **satisfies** this inequality.\n\n- We substitute the value $a=2$ into the given inequality. We get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3\\le 2a+1 & <8 \\\\ -3\\le 2\\cdot 2+1 & <8 \\\\ -3\\le 5 & <8\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, substituting $a=2$ into the inequality leads to a **true** statement. \n\nTherefore, $a=2$ **satisfies** this inequality.\n\n- We substitute the value $a=5$ into the given inequality. We get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3\\le 2a+1 & <8 \\\\ -3\\le 2\\cdot 5+1 & <8 \\\\ -3\\le 11 & <8\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, substituting $a=5$ into the inequality leads to a **false** statement. \n\nTherefore, $a=5$ **does not satisfy** this inequality." 13194139535234 17592186045417 true] [17592186069937 1064 "A radical expression like $\\sqrt{{\\color{black}x}}$ can also be written using a fractional exponent as $x^{1/2}$. They mean the same thing.\n\nSimilarly,\n\n- a cube root such as $\\sqrt[{\\color{blue}3\\!\\phantom{p}}]{x}$ is equivalent to $x^{1/\\color{blue}{3}}$,\n- a tenth root $\\sqrt[{\\color{blue}10\\!\\phantom{p}}]{x}$ is equivalent to $x^{1/\\color{blue}{10}}$,\n\nand so on. In general, we have the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[n]{x} = x^{1/n}\n$$" 13194139535234 17592186045417 false] [17592186069937 1064 "A radical expression like $\\sqrt{{\\color{black}x}}$ can also be written using a fractional exponent as $x^{1/2}.$ They mean the same thing.\n\nSimilarly,\n\n- a cube root such as $\\sqrt[{\\color{blue}3\\!\\phantom{p}}]{x}$ is equivalent to $x^{1/\\color{blue}{3}},$\n\n- a tenth root $\\sqrt[{\\color{blue}10\\!\\phantom{p}}]{x}$ is equivalent to $x^{1/\\color{blue}{10}},$\n\nand so on. In general, we have the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[n]{x} = x^{1/n}\n$$" 13194139535234 17592186045417 true] [17592186069940 1064 "Sometimes, perfect square trinomials have a leading coefficient that is something other than $1$. For example, the expression\n\n$$\n4x^2+12x+9\n$$\n\nhas a leading coefficient of $4$, and it is a perfect square trinomial because it is the square of the binomial $(2x+3)$ as we can see below:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(2x + 3)^{2} &=  \\\\\n(2x)^{2} + 2(2x)(3) + (3)^{2} &=  \\\\\n&= 4x^{2} + 12x + 9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo factor a perfect square trinomial with a leading coefficient other than $1$, we use the square of sum formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na^{2} + 2ab + b^{2} &= (a + b)^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo illustrate, let's factor the expression $4x^2+12x+9$ using the square of sum formula. We perform the following steps:\n\n1. Write both the quadratic term and the constant term as perfect squares:\n$4x^{2} + 12x + 9|=; (2x)^{2} + 12x + (3)^{2}$\n2. Factor a $2$ out of the linear term:\n$(2x)^{2} + 2(6x) + (3)^{2}$\n3. Write the middle parentheses $(6x)$ as a product of $2x$ and ${\\color{blue}3}$:\n$(2x)^{2} + 2(2x \\cdot 3) + (3)^{2}$\n4. Factor the trinomial using the square of sum formula. In this case, we have $a=2x$ and $b=3$:\n$(2x + 3)^{2}$\n\n**Note:** This method works *only* when the trinomial is a perfect square! It will *not* work for every trinomial. But we will learn more general methods in the future." 13194139535234 17592186045417 false] [17592186069940 1064 "Sometimes, perfect square trinomials have a leading coefficient that is something other than $1.$ For example, the expression\n\n$$\n4x^2+12x+9\n$$\n\nhas a leading coefficient of $4,$ and it is a perfect square trinomial because it is the square of the binomial $(2x+3)$ as we can see below:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2x+3)^{2} & = \\\\ (2x)^{2}+2(2x)(3)+(3)^{2} & = \\\\ 4{x}^{2}+12x+9 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo factor a perfect square trinomial with a leading coefficient other than $1,$ we use the square of sum formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}^{2}+2ab+{b}^{2} & =(a+b)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo illustrate, let's factor the expression $4x^2+12x+9$ using the square of sum formula. We perform the following steps:\n\n1\\. Write both the quadratic term and the constant term as perfect squares:\n\n  $$\n  \\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}+12x+9 & = \\\\ (2x)^{2}+12x+(3)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n  $$\n\n2\\. Factor a $2$ out of the linear term:\n\n  $$\n  \\begin{aligned}(2x)^{2}+2(6x)+(3)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n  $$\n\n3\\. Write the middle parentheses $(6x)$ as a product of $2x$ and ${\\color{blue}3}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n  $$\n  \\begin{aligned}(2x)^{2}+2(2x\\cdot 3)+(3)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n  $$\n\n4\\. Factor the trinomial using the square of sum formula. In this case, we have $a=2x$ and $b=3\\mathbin{:}$\n\n  $$\n  \\begin{aligned}(2x+3)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n  $$\n\n**Note:** This method works *only* when the trinomial is a perfect square! It will *not* work for every trinomial. But we will learn more general methods in the future." 13194139535234 17592186045417 true] [17592186069941 1064 "How can we factor a trinomial that has a negative leading coefficient, like\n\n$$\n-25y^2 + 20y - 4\\,?\n$$\n\n \nWe can reduce this to a more familiar problem by factoring out $−1$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-(25y^2 - 20y + 4)\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the quadratic expression inside the parentheses using the square of a difference formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}25{y}^{2}-20y+4 & = \\\\ (5y)^{2}-20y+{2}^{2} & = \\\\ (5y)^{2}-2(10y)+{2}^{2} & = \\\\ (5y)^{2}-2(5y\\cdot 2)+{2}^{2} & = \\\\ (5y-2)^{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our original expression factors as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-(25{y}^{2}-20y+4) & = \\\\ -(5y-2)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535234 17592186045417 true] [17592186069941 1064 "How can we factor a trinomial that has a negative leading coefficient, like\n$-25y^2 + 20y - 4\\,$?\nWe can reduce this to a more familiar problem by factoring out $-1$ from the entire expression:\n$-(25y^2 - 20y + 4)$\n\nNow, we factor the quadratic expression inside the parentheses using the square of a difference formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n25y^{2} - 20y + 4 &=  \\\\\n(5y)^{2} - 20y + 2^{2} &=  \\\\\n(5y)^{2} - 2(10y) + 2^{2} &=  \\\\\n(5y)^{2} - 2(5y \\cdot 2) + 2^{2} &=  \\\\\n&= (5y - 2)^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our original expression factors as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n- (25y^{2} - 20y + 4) &=  \\\\\n&= - (5y - 2)^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535234 17592186045417 false] [17592186069942 1064 "To find the **cube root** of a number $x$, we must ask ourselves, \"what number *cubed* equals $x$?\"\n\nFor example, the cube root of $8$ is $2$ since\n\n$$\n2^3 = 2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 2 = 8\n$$\n\nand the cube root of $-8$ is $-2$ since\n\n$$\n(-2)^3 = (-2) \\cdot (-2) \\cdot (-2) = -8\n$$\n\nIn general, the cube root of $x^3$ is $x$.\n\nLike the square root, the cube root is signified by a radical symbol, but it also has a little $3$ next to it, i.e., $\\sqrt[3\\!\\phantom{p}]{\\phantom{2}}$. Using this notation, we can express the above statements mathematically:\n\n- \"The cube root of $8$ is $2$\" can be expressed as $\\sqrt[3]{8}=2$.\n- \"The cube root of $-8$ is $-2$\" can be expressed as $\\sqrt[3]{-8}=-2$.\n- \"The cube root of $x^3$ is $x$\" can be expressed as $\\sqrt[3]{x^3}=x$.\n\n**Note:** For square roots, we have\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{x^2} = \\mid x \\mid\n$$\n\nbut for cube roots, we have\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[3]{x^3} = x\n$$\n\nNo absolute value is needed for cube roots, because\n\n- the cube root of a positive number is a positive number, and\n- the cube root of a negative number is a negative number." 13194139535234 17592186045417 false] [17592186069942 1064 "To find the **cube root** of a number $x$, we must ask ourselves, \"what number *cubed* equals $x$?\"\n\nFor example, the cube root of $8$ is $2$ since \n\n$$\n2^3 = 2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 2 = 8,\n$$\n\nand the cube root of $-8$ is $-2$ since\n\n$$\n(-2)^3 = (-2) \\cdot (-2) \\cdot (-2) = -8.\n$$\n\nIn general, the cube root of $x^3$ is $x.$\n\nLike the square root, the cube root is signified by a radical symbol, but it also has a little $3$ next to it, i.e., $\\sqrt[3\\!\\phantom{p}]{\\phantom{2}}.$ Using this notation, we can express the above statements mathematically:\n\n- \"The cube root of $8$ is $2$\" can be expressed as $\\sqrt[3]{8}=2.$\n\n- \"The cube root of $-8$ is $-2$\" can be expressed as  $\\sqrt[3]{-8}=-2.$\n\n- \"The cube root of $x^3$ is $x$\" can be expressed as  $\\sqrt[3]{x^3}=x.$\n\n**Note:** For square roots, we have $\\sqrt{x^2} = |x|,$ but for cube roots, we have $\\sqrt[3]{x^3} = x.$ No absolute value is needed for cube roots, because\n\n- the cube root of a positive number is a positive number, and\n\n- the cube root of a negative number is a negative number." 13194139535234 17592186045417 true] [17592186069954 1064 "Other perfect square trinomials can be factored as the square of a difference:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na^{2} - 2ab + b^{2} &= (a - b)^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo illustrate, let's factor the expression $9x^2-24x+16$ using the square of difference formula. We perform the following steps:\n\n1. Write both the quadratic term and the constant term as perfect squares:\n$9x^{2} - 24x + 16|=; (3x)^{2} - 24x + (4)^{2}$\n2. Factor a $2$ out of the linear term:\n$(3x)^{2} - 24x + (4)^{2}|=; (3x)^{2} - 2(12x) + (4)^{2}$\n3. Write the middle parentheses $(12x)$ as a product of $3x$ and ${\\color{blue}4}$:\n$(3x)^{2} - 2(12x) + (4)^{2}|=; (3x)^{2} - 2(3x \\cdot 4) + (4)^{2}$\n4. Factor the trinomial using the square of difference formula. In this case, we have $a=3x$ and $b=4$:\n$(3x)^{2} - 2(3x \\cdot 4) + (4)^{2}|=; (3x - 4)^{2}$\n\n**Note:** Once again, we note that this method works *only* when the trinomial is a perfect square! It will *not* work for every trinomial." 13194139535234 17592186045417 false] [17592186069954 1064 "Other perfect square trinomials can be factored as the square of a difference:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}^{2}-2ab+{b}^{2} & =(a-b)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo illustrate, let's factor the expression $9x^2-24x+16$ using the square of difference formula. We perform the following steps:\n\n1\\. Write both the quadratic term and the constant term as perfect squares:\n\n  $$\n  \\begin{aligned}9{x}^{2}-24x+16 & = \\\\ (3x)^{2}-24x+(4)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n  $$\n\n2\\. Factor a $2$ out of the linear term:\n\n  $$\n  \\begin{aligned}(3x)^{2}-24x+(4)^{2} & = \\\\ (3x)^{2}-2(12x)+(4)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n  $$\n\n3\\. Write the middle parentheses $(12x)$ as a product of $3x$ and ${\\color{blue}4}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n  $$\n  \\begin{aligned}(3x)^{2}-2(12x)+(4)^{2} & = \\\\ (3x)^{2}-2(3x\\cdot 4)+(4)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n  $$\n\n4\\. Factor the trinomial using the square of difference formula. In this case, we have $a=3x$ and $b=4\\mathbin{:}$\n\n  $$\n  \\begin{aligned}(3x)^{2}-2(3x\\cdot 4)+(4)^{2} & = \\\\ (3x-4)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n  $$\n\n**Note:** Once again, we note that this method works *only* when the trinomial is a perfect square! It will *not* work for every trinomial." 13194139535234 17592186045417 true] [17592186069979 1064 "We can find which values satisfy an inequality by trial and error, which involves substituting a number for the variable and checking to see if we get a true statement.\n\nFor example, which of the following values of $y$ satisfy the inequality $y - 1 > 3\\,$?\n\n1. $y=6$\n2. $y=2$\n3. $y=3$\n\nLet's go through the possibilities one at a time.\n\n- Substituting the value ${\\color{blue}y}={\\color{blue}6}$ into the given equation, we get:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}y - 1 & > 3 \\\\ 6 - 1 & > 3 \\\\ 5 & > 3✓\\end{bmatrix}$\nThis is a **true** statement, so $y=6$ **satisfies** this inequality.\n- Substituting the value ${\\color{blue}y}={\\color{blue}2}$ into the given equation, we get:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}y - 1 & > 3 \\\\ 2 - 1 & > 3 \\\\ 1 & > 3\\cdot\\end{bmatrix}$\nThis is a **false** statement, so $y=2$ **does not satisfy** this inequality.\n- Substituting the value ${\\color{blue}y}={\\color{blue}3}$ into the given equation, we get:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}y - 1 & > 3 \\\\ 3 - 1 & > 3 \\\\ 2 & > 3\\cdot\\end{bmatrix}$\nThis is a **false** statement, so $y=3$ **does not satisfy** this inequality.\n\nTherefore, only I satisfies the inequality." 13194139535234 17592186045417 false] [17592186069979 1064 "We can find which values satisfy an inequality by trial and error, which involves substituting a number for the variable and checking to see if we get a true statement.\n\nFor example, which of the following values of $y$ satisfy the inequality $y - 1 > 3\\,?$\n\nI. $y=6$\n\nII. $y=2$\n\nIII. $y=3$\n\nLet's go through the possibilities one at a time.\n\n- Substituting the value ${\\color{blue}y}={\\color{blue}6}$ into the given equation, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-1 & >3 \\\\ 6-1 & >3 \\\\ 5 & >3\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a **true** statement, so $y=6$ **satisfies** this inequality.\n\n- Substituting the value ${\\color{blue}y}={\\color{blue}2}$ into the given equation, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-1 & >3 \\\\ 2-1 & >3 \\\\ 1 & >3\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a **false** statement, so $y=2$ **does not satisfy** this inequality.\n\n- Substituting the value ${\\color{blue}y}={\\color{blue}3}$ into the given equation, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-1 & >3 \\\\ 3-1 & >3 \\\\ 2 & >3\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a **false** statement, so $y=3$ **does not satisfy** this inequality.\n\nTherefore, only I satisfies the inequality." 13194139535234 17592186045417 true] [17592186073879 1038 #edb/ref 17592186484232 13194139535234 17592186045417 true] [17592186073879 1038 #edb/ref 17592186484255 13194139535234 17592186045417 true] [17592186073879 1038 #edb/ref 17592186484280 13194139535234 17592186045417 true] [17592186073880 1038 #edb/ref 17592186484228 13194139535234 17592186045417 true] [17592186073880 1038 #edb/ref 17592186484230 13194139535234 17592186045417 true] [17592186100928 1038 #edb/ref 17592186484203 13194139535234 17592186045417 true] [17592186100937 1038 #edb/ref 17592186484243 13194139535234 17592186045417 true] [17592186100937 1038 #edb/ref 17592186484284 13194139535234 17592186045417 true] [17592186102150 1078 #edb/ref 17592186102155 13194139535234 17592186045417 true] [17592186102446 1078 #edb/ref 17592186102450 13194139535234 17592186045417 true] [17592186104002 1078 #edb/ref 17592186104005 13194139535234 17592186045417 true] [17592186105907 1038 #edb/ref 17592186484224 13194139535234 17592186045417 true] [17592186105907 1038 #edb/ref 17592186484248 13194139535234 17592186045417 true] [17592186105908 1038 #edb/ref 17592186484229 13194139535234 17592186045417 true] [17592186106044 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535234 17592186045417 true] [17592186106044 1071 "Calculate the volume of the parallelepiped spanned by $AB^(→)$, $AC^(→)$ and $AD^(→)$, given the points $A(9, 6, 1)$, $B(9, 7, 2)$, $C(7, 5, 2)$, and $D(7, 8, 3)$." 13194139535234 17592186045417 false] [17592186106044 1071 "Calculate the volume of the parallelepiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD},$ given the points $A(9,6,1),$ $B(9,7,2),$ $C(7,5,2),$ and $D(7,8,3).$" 13194139535234 17592186045417 true] [17592186106044 1074 "First, we find the displacement vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ that span the parallelepiped:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(9i+7j+2k)-(9i+6j+k) \\\\  & =j+k \\\\  & =⟨0,1,1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(7i+5j+2k)-(9i+6j+k) \\\\  & =-2i-j+k \\\\  & =⟨-2,-1,1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AD} & =d-a \\\\  & =(7i+8j+3k)-(9i+6j+k) \\\\  & =-2i+2j+2k \\\\  & =⟨-2,2,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the volume of the parallelepiped, we need to take the absolute value of the triple product of the above vectors.\n\nComputing the triple product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot (\\overset{\\to }{AC}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AD}) & =|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =0\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}|-1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}|+1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =0-((-2)\\cdot 2-(-2)\\cdot 1)+((-2)\\cdot 2-(-2)\\cdot (-1)) \\\\  & =2-6 \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the corresponding parallelepiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ is\n\n$$\nV=|-4|=4.\n$$" 13194139535234 17592186045417 true] [17592186106095 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535234 17592186045417 true] [17592186106095 1071 "Find $t$ given that the vectors $p = ⟨3, t, t⟩$, $q = ⟨1, 3, 1⟩$, and $r = ⟨1, 1, 3⟩$ are coplanar." 13194139535234 17592186045417 false] [17592186106095 1071 "Find $t$ given that the vectors $p=⟨3,t,t⟩$, $q=⟨1,3,1⟩$, and $r=⟨1,1,3⟩$ are coplanar." 13194139535234 17592186045417 true] [17592186106095 1074 "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\cdot (q\\times r) & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & t & t \\\\ 1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}|-t\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}|+t\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3(3\\cdot 3-1\\cdot 1)-t(1\\cdot 3-1\\cdot 1)+t(1\\cdot 1-3\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =24-2t-2t \\\\  & =24-4t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\cdot (q\\times r) & =0 \\\\ 24-4t & =0 \\\\ t & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $t=6.$" 13194139535234 17592186045417 true] [17592186106309 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535234 17592186045417 true] [17592186106309 1071 "Calculate the volume of the parallelepiped spanned by $x = ⟨3, 5, 0⟩$, $y = ⟨3, 2, 0⟩$ and $z = ⟨3, 4, 5⟩$." 13194139535234 17592186045417 false] [17592186106309 1071 "Calculate the volume of the parallelepiped spanned by $x=⟨3,5,0⟩$, $y=⟨3,2,0⟩$ and $z=⟨3,4,5⟩.$" 13194139535234 17592186045417 true] [17592186106309 1074 "The volume of the parallelepiped spanned by three vectors is given by the absolute value of their triple product.\n\nFirst, we compute the triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot (y\\times z) & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 5\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3|\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 5\\end{aligned}|-5|\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{aligned}|+0|\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3(2\\cdot 5-4\\cdot 0)-5(3\\cdot 5-3\\cdot 0)+0 \\\\  & =30-75 \\\\  & =-45\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the absolute value. So the volume of the corresponding parallelepiped is\n\n$$\nV=|-45|=45.\n$$" 13194139535234 17592186045417 true] [17592186119778 1038 #edb/ref 17592186484199 13194139535234 17592186045417 true] [17592186119778 1038 #edb/ref 17592186484234 13194139535234 17592186045417 true] [17592186119779 1038 #edb/ref 17592186484198 13194139535234 17592186045417 true] [17592186119780 1038 #edb/ref 17592186484211 13194139535234 17592186045417 true] [17592186119780 1038 #edb/ref 17592186484286 13194139535234 17592186045417 true] [17592186119786 1038 #edb/ref 17592186484208 13194139535234 17592186045417 true] [17592186119786 1038 #edb/ref 17592186484275 13194139535234 17592186045417 true] [17592186119838 1038 #edb/ref 17592186