5-2(-2)=9 \\\\ y=-1+4(-2)=-9 \\\\ z=-3-5(-2)=7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection point is  $P(9,-9,7).$" 13194139535234 17592186045417 false] [17592186120545 1068 #edb/ref 17592186119383 13194139535234 17592186045417 false] [17592186124656 1038 #edb/ref 17592186484220 13194139535234 17592186045417 true] [17592186124656 1038 #edb/ref 17592186484239 13194139535234 17592186045417 true] [17592186125035 1071 "Find the global extrema of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4x+4$ on the closed region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 9\\}.$ You are given that the function has a critical point at $(2,0)\\in D.$" 13194139535234 17592186045417 true] [17592186125035 1071 "Find the global extrema of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4x+4$ on the closed region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 9}.$ You are given that the function has a critical point at $(2,0)\\in D.$" 13194139535234 17592186045417 false] [17592186133468 1071 "![](images/b3/b36961dd90e73504bf46d63827d1ea68292c01a7990f8fa3eb00eee62a4b1813.svg)\n\nConsider the matrix given above. Identify **all** of the reasons why one can conclude that $|A|=0.$\n\n- $A$ has a zero column\n\n- $A$ has proportional rows\n\n- $A$ has identical columns" 13194139535234 17592186045417 false] [17592186133468 1071 "![](images/b3/b36961dd90e73504bf46d63827d1ea68292c01a7990f8fa3eb00eee62a4b1813.svg)\n\nConsider the matrix given above. Identify **all** of the reasons why one can conclude that $|A|=0.$\n\nI. $A$ has a zero column\n\nII. $A$ has proportional rows\n\nIII. $A$ has identical columns" 13194139535234 17592186045417 true] [17592186133911 1038 #edb/ref 17592186484213 13194139535234 17592186045417 true] [17592186133911 1038 #edb/ref 17592186484278 13194139535234 17592186045417 true] [17592186133912 1038 #edb/ref 17592186484193 13194139535234 17592186045417 true] [17592186135154 1064 "In conclusion, if an $n\\!\\times \\!n$ matrix $A$ satisfies any one of the following conditions, then $\\det(A)=0.$\n\n- $A$ has a zero row/column\n\n- $A$ has two identical rows/columns\n\n- $A$ has two proportional rows/columns\n\n- $A$ has a row/column that is a linear combination of other rows/columns\n\nNotice that condition IV encompasses each of the conditions I, II, and III. If we have a zero row/column, two identical rows/columns, or proportional rows/columns, then the rows/columns form a linearly dependent set of vectors. \nIn turn, this means that one of the rows/columns is a linear combination of other rows/columns.\n\n**Watch out!** While all of the above conditions are *sufficient* to conclude that $\\det(A)=0,$ only condition IV is *necessary* to conclude that $\\det(A)=0.$ \nIn other words, if we have a matrix $A$ with $\\det(A)=0,$ then conditions I, II, and III may or may not be satisfied, while condition IV *must* be satisfied. \n\nSo, we have the following theorem:\n\n*$\\det(A)=0$ if and only if one of the rows/columns of $A$ is a linear combination of other rows/columns of $A.$*\n\nFinally, note also that in the case of a $2\\times 2$ matrix *only*, statements III and IV mean the same thing." 13194139535234 17592186045417 false] [17592186135154 1064 "In conclusion, if an $n\\!\\times \\!n$ matrix $A$ satisfies any one of the following conditions, then $\\det(A)=0.$\n\nI. $A$ has a zero row/column\n\nII. $A$ has two identical rows/columns\n\nIII. $A$ has two proportional rows/columns\n\nIV. $A$ has a row/column that is a linear combination of other rows/columns\n\nNotice that condition IV encompasses each of the conditions I, II, and III. If we have a zero row/column, two identical rows/columns, or proportional rows/columns, then the rows/columns form a linearly dependent set of vectors. \nIn turn, this means that one of the rows/columns is a linear combination of other rows/columns.\n\n**Watch out!** While all of the above conditions are *sufficient* to conclude that $\\det(A)=0,$ only condition IV is *necessary* to conclude that $\\det(A)=0.$ \nIn other words, if we have a matrix $A$ with $\\det(A)=0,$ then conditions I, II, and III may or may not be satisfied, while condition IV *must* be satisfied. \n\nSo, we have the following theorem:\n\n*$\\det(A)=0$ if and only if one of the rows/columns of $A$ is a linear combination of other rows/columns of $A.$*\n\nFinally, note also that in the case of a $2\\times 2$ matrix *only*, statements III and IV mean the same thing." 13194139535234 17592186045417 true] [17592186152030 1071 "Which of the following vector spaces has a dimension of $9?$\n\n- $\\mathbb{R}^8$\n\n- $\\mathbb{R}_8[t]$, the vector space of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $8$\n\n- $\\text{M}_{3}(\\mathbb{R}),$ the vector space of all $3 \\times 3$ matrices over $\\mathbb{R}$" 13194139535234 17592186045417 false] [17592186152030 1071 "Which of the following vector spaces has a dimension of $9?$\n\nI. $\\mathbb{R}^8$\n\nII. $\\mathbb{R}_8[t]$, the vector space of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $8$\n\nIII. $\\text{M}_{3}(\\mathbb{R}),$ the vector space of all $3 \\times 3$ matrices over $\\mathbb{R}$" 13194139535234 17592186045417 true] [17592186152030 1074 "Let's examine each of the vector spaces in turn.\n\n- Recall that $\\textrm{dim}(\\mathbb{R}^n)=n.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\textrm{dim}(\\mathbb{R}^8)=8\\neq 9.\\quad {\\color{red}{\\times}}\n$$\n\n- Recall that $\\textrm{dim}(\\mathbb{R}_{n}[t])=n+1.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\textrm{dim}(\\mathbb{R}_{8}[t])=8+1=9.\\quad {\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}\n$$\n\n- Recall that $\\textrm{dim}(\\text{M}_{n}(\\mathbb{R}))=n^2.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}(\\text{M}_{3}(\\mathbb{R}))=3^2=9.\\quad {\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}\n$$\n\nHence, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139535234 17592186045417 false] [17592186152030 1074 "Let's examine each of the vector spaces in turn.\n\nI. Recall that $\\textrm{dim}(\\mathbb{R}^n)=n.$ Therefore, \n\n  $$\n  \\textrm{dim}(\\mathbb{R}^8)=8\\neq 9.\\quad {\\color{red}{\\times}}\n  $$\n\nII. Re