s back into the equations of the line.\n\n- For $t=0$, we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=3(0)+3=3 \\\\ y=(0)+1=1 \\\\ z=(0)+1=1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the first point is $(3,1,1).$\n\n- For $t=-2$, we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=3(-2)+3=-3 \\\\ y=(-2)+1=-1 \\\\ z=(-2)+1=-1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the second point is $(-3,-1,-1).$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $(3,1,1)$ and $(-3,-1,-1).$" 13194139535234 17592186045417 true] [17592186198998 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535234 17592186045417 true] [17592186198998 1071 "Find the point(s) of the intersection of the ellipsoid $\\frac{x^{2}}{2} + \\frac{y^{2}}{3} + \\frac{z^{2}}{6} = 1$ and the line\n${x = t; y = 1 - t,; z = t + 2$ where $t ∈ (- ∞, ∞)$." 13194139535234 17592186045417 false] [17592186198998 1071 "Find the point(s) of the intersection of the ellipsoid $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{6}=1$ and the line \n$\\{\\begin{aligned}x=t \\\\ y=1-t, \\\\ z=t+2\\end{aligned}$ where $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$" 13194139535234 17592186045417 true] [17592186198998 1074 "Substituting the expressions for $x$, $y$, and $z$ from the parametric equation of the line into the equation of the ellipsoid, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{6} & =1 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =6 \\\\ 3(t)^{2}+2(1-t)^{2}+(t+2)^{2} & =6 \\\\ 3{t}^{2}+2(1-2t+{t}^{2})+({t}^{2}+4t+4) & =6 \\\\ 6{t}^{2}+6 & =6 \\\\ 6{t}^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the solution $t=0.$ \n\nNow, we substitute our solution $t=0$ back into the equations of the line.\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=0 \\\\ y=1-(0)=1 \\\\ z=(0)+2=2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the point is $(0,1,2).$\n\nTherefore, the point of intersection is $(0,1,2).$" 13194139535234 17592186045417 true] [17592186199499 1064 "Now, let's consider the intersection of the elliptic paraboloid \n$z=x^2+y^2$\nand the plane \n$z = x+y-\\dfrac{1}{4},$\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/1d/1d4b8db0985d4