the system. The resulting projection curve will be an ellipse, as depicted below.\n\n![](images/05/059acf1ec5dadaa98b2cd4d4b6c79884e4facc96dd49664abbc247cfba86aaeb.png)\n\n- To find the projection of the intersection onto the $yz$-plane, we need to eliminate the variable $x$ from the system. The resulting projection curve will be an ellipse, as depicted below.\n\n![](images/80/80d3042d21d2e7265a40739ae1531ac30b03fea16fa67609a4e1b0eac8559411.png)\n\nLet's see some examples." 13194139535234 17592186045417 false] [17592186199500 1064 "Suppose we wish to find the points of intersection of the elliptic paraboloid \n$z=x^2+y^2$\nand the line with the equation\n$x = -y = -z+1.$\nThe line, paraboloid, and their points of intersection are shown below.\n\n![](images/51/5159ac99a1bc9f73c01df3c2cc2e6fb2093a3d68e00731609d7b62a6fa55149d.png)\n\n \n\nThe trick is to express the equation of the line in parametric form, substitute the parametric equations into the equation of the surface, and solve for the parameter.\n\nSo, first, we write the equation of our line in the parametric form:\n\n![](images/01/01357a5c6b72d7c976765aeed04db8afa3e3a80e13095d9be1ea2f2594ae5bf5.svg)\n\nwhere $t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$\n\nOur intersection points must belong to the line and the surface simultaneously. So, substituting the expressions for $x$, $y$, and $z$ from the parametric equation of the line into the equation of the paraboloid, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2} \\\\ (-t+1) & =(t)^{2}+(-t)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimplifying this equation, we get\n\n$$\n2t^2+t - 1=0.\n$$\n\nThe solutions to this equation are\n$t=\\dfrac{1}{2}$ and $t=-1.$ \n\nSubstituting these values back into the parametric equations of the line\n\n- For $t=\\dfrac{1}{2}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=(\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ y=-(\\frac{1}{2})=-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ z=-(\\frac{1}{2})+1=\\frac{1}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the first point is $(\\dfrac{1}{2},-\\dfrac{1}{2},\\dfrac{1}{2}).$\n\n- For $t=-1$, we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=(-1)=-1 \\\\ y=-(-1)=1 \\\\ z=-(-1)+1=2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the second point is $(-1,1,2).$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $(\\dfrac{1}{2},-\\dfrac{1}{2},\\dfrac{1}{2})$ and $(-1,1,2).$ \n\nLet's see another example." 13194139535234 17592186045417 true] [17592186199500 1064 "Suppose we wish to find the points of intersection of the elliptic paraboloid \n$z=x^2+y^2$\nand the line with the equation\n$x = -y = -z+1.$\nThe line, paraboloid, and their points of intersection are shown below.\n\n![](images/51/5159ac99a1bc9f73c01df3c2cc2e6fb2093a3d68e00731609d7b62a6fa55149d.png)\n\nThe trick is to express the equation of the line in parametric form, substitute the parametric equations into the equation of the surface, and solve for the parameter.\n\nSo, first, we write the equation of our line in the parametric form:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=t \\\\ y=-t \\\\ z=-t+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$\n\nOur intersection points must belong to the line and the surface simultaneously. So, substituting the expressions for $x$, $y$, and $z$ from the parametric equation of the line into the equation of the paraboloid, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2} \\\\ (-t+1) & =(t)^{2}+(-t)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimplifying this equation, we get\n\n$$\n2t^2+t - 1=0.\n$$\n\nThe solutions to this equation are\n$t=\\dfrac{1}{2}$ and $t=-1.$ \n\nSubstituting these values back into the parametric equations of the line\n\n- For $t=\\dfrac{1}{2}$, we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=(\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ y=-(\\frac{1}{2})=-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ z=-(\\frac{1}{2})+1=\\frac{1}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the first point is $(\\dfrac{1}{2},-\\dfrac{1}{2},\\dfrac{1}{2}).$\n\n- For $t=-1$, we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=(-1)=-1 \\\\ y=-(-1)=1 \\\\ z=-(-1)+1=2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the second point is $(-1,1,2).$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $(\\dfrac{1}{2},-\\dfrac{1}{2},\\dfrac{1}{2})$ and $(-1,1,2).$ \n\nLet's see another example." 13194139535234 17592186045417 false] [17592186221456 1038 #edb/ref 17592186484247 13194139535234 17592186045417 true] [17592186221456 1038 #edb/ref 17592186484267 13194139535234 17592186045417 true] [17592186243971 1074 "The standard basis of ${R}_{n}[t]$ is\n\n$$\n\\{1,\\,t,\\,{t}^{2},\\,…,\\,{t}^{n}\\}.\n$$\n\nSo, the standard basis of ${R}_{24}[t]$ is \n\n$$\n\\{1,\\,t,\\,{t}^{2},\\,…,\\,{t}^{24}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({R}_{24}[t])=24+1=25.\n$$\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({R}_{n}[t])=n+1.\n$$" 13194139535234 17592186045417 true] [17592186243971 1074 "The standard basis of ${R}_{n}[t]$ is\n\n$$\n{1,\\,t,\\,{t}^{2},\\,…,\\,{t}^{n}}.\n$$\n\nSo, the standard basis of ${R}_{24}[t]$ is \n\n$$\n{1,\\,t,\\,{t}^{2},\\,…,\\,{t}^{24}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({R}_{24}[t])=24+1=25.\n$$\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({R}_{n}[t])=n+1.\n$$" 13194139535234 17592186045417 false] [17592186289935 1075 #edb/ref 17592186483587 13194139535234 17592186045417 true] [17592186289935 1078 #edb/ref 17592186288333 13194139535234 17592186045417 false] [17592186289935 1078 #edb/ref 17592186483587 13194139535234 17592186045417 true] [17592186317450 1074 "We assume that a solution of our equation has the following Fourier series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x))∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}n\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x) \\\\ {y}_{p}^{″} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}n\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x))∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{b}_{n}{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{p}^{″}$ and ${y}_{p}$ into the equation and have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{b}_{n}{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x)-8(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x))=8+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{3}\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nRearranging the terms, we get \n\n$$\n-8\\cdot \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}-8)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x)=8+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{3}\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the free terms and sine coefficients, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-8\\cdot \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}=8 \\\\ {b}_{n}(-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}-8)=\\frac{3(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{3}\\pi }\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}=-1 \\\\ {b}_{n}=\\frac{3(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{3}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+8)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼-1+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{3(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{3}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+8)}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$" 13194139535234 17592186045417 true] [17592186317450 1074 "We assume that a solution of our equation has the following Fourier series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x))∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}n\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x) \\\\ {y}_{p}^{″} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}n\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x))∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{b}_{n}{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{p}^{″}$ and ${y}_{p}$ into the equation and have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{b}_{n}{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x)-8(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x))=8+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{3}\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nRearranging the terms, we get \n\n$$\n-8\\cdot \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}-8)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x)=8+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{3}\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the free terms and sine coefficients, we obtain:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-8\\cdot \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}=8 \\\\ {b}_{n}(-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}-8)=\\frac{3(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{3}\\pi }\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}=-1 \\\\ {b}_{n}=\\frac{3(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{3}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+8)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼-1+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{3(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{3}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+8)}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$" 13194139535234 17592186045417 false] [17592186317453 1074 "We begin by identifying resonance. The differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\pi }^{2}y=f(x)\n$$\n\nhas a natural frequency of \n\n$$\n\\omega =\\sqrt{{\\pi }^{2}}=\\pi .\n$$\n\nThe frequencies of the forcing harmonics for a $4$-periodic function $(L=2)$ are \n\n$$\n{\\omega }_{n}=\\frac{n\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nFor $n=2$, we have ${\\omega }_{2}=\\pi .$ Since ${\\omega }_{2}=\\omega ,$ the $n=2$ harmonic of the forcing function causes resonance.\n\nFor the $n=2$ harmonic, the forcing term is \n\n$$\n{f}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x).\n$$\n\nBecause this frequency matches the natural frequency, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{2}}=x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{{p}_{2}}^{′} & =(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x))+x(-\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)+\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)) \\\\ {y}_{{p}_{2}}^{″} & =-2\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)+2\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)-{\\pi }^{2}x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{{p}_{2}}^{″}$ and ${y}_{{p}_{2}}$ into the equation ${y}^{″}+{\\pi }^{2}y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x):$\n\n$$\n[-2\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)+2\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)-{\\pi }^{2}{y}_{{p}_{2}}]+{\\pi }^{2}{y}_{{p}_{2}}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)\n$$\n\nThe resonant terms involving $x$ cancel out, leaving\n\n$$\n-2\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)+2\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the sine and cosine coefficients, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-2\\pi A=1 \\\\ 2\\pi B=0\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}A=-\\frac{1}{2\\pi } \\\\ B=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resonant component of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{2}}=-\\frac{x}{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x).\n$$" 13194139535234 17592186045417 true] [17592186317453 1074 "We begin by identifying resonance. The differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\pi }^{2}y=f(x)\n$$\n\nhas a natural frequency of \n\n$$\n\\omega =\\sqrt{{\\pi }^{2}}=\\pi .\n$$\n\nThe frequencies of the forcing harmonics for a $4$-periodic function $(L=2)$ are \n\n$$\n{\\omega }_{n}=\\frac{n\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nFor $n=2$, we have ${\\omega }_{2}=\\pi .$ Since ${\\omega }_{2}=\\omega ,$ the $n=2$ harmonic of the forcing function causes resonance.\n\nFor the $n=2$ harmonic, the forcing term is \n\n$$\n{f}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x).\n$$\n\nBecause this frequency matches the natural frequency, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{2}}=x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{{p}_{2}}^{′} & =(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x))+x(-\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)+\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)) \\\\ {y}_{{p}_{2}}^{″